# Rotating credentials

Each credential on your stack has its own rotation surface. There
is no master "rotate everything" button -- and no central vault
file -- because each app owns its own credential and rotating it
in one place is enough.

This page lists every credential, where it lives, and how to
rotate it. None of these flows require operator action: you
control all the surfaces below directly.

## Per-credential rotation matrix

### SMTP relay password (Keycloak emails, etc.)

- **Where it lives**: Dokploy admin UI (`apps.{{ cloudflare_zone }}`)
  -> Keycloak compose -> Environment tab -> `SMTP_PASSWORD`.
- **How to rotate**: mint a new SMTP credential at your provider
  (Brevo / Resend / SES). Edit `SMTP_PASSWORD` in the Environment
  tab. Click `Redeploy` on the Keycloak service. Test with
  Keycloak admin UI -> Realm settings -> Email -> `Test connection`.
- **Operator-side?** No. Your edit survives the next operator
  converge -- the catena role uses a "reconcile-not-overwrite"
  filter that preserves credential-shaped env vars when they are
  already set.

### App admin / database passwords (Nextcloud admin, etc.)

- **Where it lives**: Dokploy admin UI -> the app's compose ->
  Environment tab.
- **How to rotate**: same flow as SMTP. Edit the env var,
  redeploy.

### OIDC client secrets

- **Where it lives**: Keycloak admin UI (`auth.{{ cloudflare_zone }}`)
  -> Clients -> the relevant client -> Credentials.
- **How to rotate**: click `Regenerate Secret`, copy the new
  value, paste into the consuming app's Dokploy env. Redeploy
  the app.

### Cloudflare API token

- **Where it lives**: in your password manager (1Password,
  Bitwarden, etc.). Catena does NOT store your CF API token --
  it is consumed once at install + each tunnel regeneration,
  never persisted on the VPS or in our portal.
- **How to rotate**:
  1. Mint a new token at
     [dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens](https://dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens).
     Scope: Zone -> DNS:Edit + Zone:Read on `{{ cloudflare_zone }}`.
  2. Save in your password manager.
  3. Open the portal at `/installations/<id>/regenerate-tunnel`,
     paste the new token, click `Regenerate tunnel`. The portal
     deletes the old tunnel and creates a new one in your CF
     account using your new token. cloudflared on the VPS
     restarts with the new tunnel JSON.
  4. Delete the old token at Cloudflare once the regeneration
     succeeds.

### Tailscale per-node key (operations access)

- **Where it lives**: tailscaled state on the VPS (you don't
  edit this directly).
- **How to rotate**: from the Tailscale dashboard, expire the
  node key for this VPS. The VPS automatically re-authenticates
  on its next tailnet check-in (DIY tier: with your OAuth
  client; Managed tier: with the operator's). No portal
  involvement.

### S3 backup keys (restic)

- **Where it lives**: `/etc/catena/backup.env` on the VPS (mode
  0600 root).
- **Routine rotation**: do not. Long-lived IAM keys are the norm
  for backup workloads.
- **Incident response**: if you have a confirmed compromise,
  open a `requests` row in the portal asking the operator to
  rotate. Operator runs the
  [rotate-s3-backup-keys runbook](https://docs.catena.run/runbooks/rotate-s3-backup-keys)
  on your behalf.

### Restic password

- **Where it lives**: `/etc/catena/restic-password` on the VPS
  (mode 0600 root).
- **Routine rotation**: do not. The restic password is the
  symmetric key for the entire backup repo; a botched rotation
  can leave snapshots unreadable.
- **Incident response**: open a `requests` row in the portal.
  The operator follows the multi-step add/verify/remove runbook.
  Coordinate timing -- do not rotate while a restore is in
  flight.

## Backup-vault export (cold storage)

For a one-time snapshot of every credential currently on your
VPS, encrypted with a passphrase you provide:

1. Sign into OliveTin at `actions.{{ cloudflare_zone }}`.
2. Click `Export recovery secrets (encrypted)`.
3. Paste a passphrase you will remember.
4. Download the resulting `.gpg` file from the link OliveTin
   provides.
5. Store the file in cold storage (offline drive, USB key,
   physical safe) alongside the passphrase in your password
   manager.

This is recovery insurance. It is NOT a routine backup -- the
nightly restic snapshots cover that. Re-export only when you
think your VPS may be lost AND your restic credentials may
ALSO be lost (provider account compromised, multi-region
outage).

## What to keep in your password manager

Per the catena security model, you are the source of truth for
every operator-impossible-to-replace credential. Save these in a
password manager you control:

- Cloudflare API token (zone-scoped to `{{ cloudflare_zone }}`).
- VPS provider API key (OVH / Hetzner / etc.).
- SMTP relay credentials (Brevo / Resend username + password).
- S3 backup IAM access key + secret (DIY tier only -- Managed
  tier creds live operator-side).
- Tailscale OAuth client + secret (DIY tier only).
- Bookmark for `apps.{{ cloudflare_zone }}` (Dokploy admin),
  `actions.{{ cloudflare_zone }}` (OliveTin), `auth.{{ cloudflare_zone }}`
  (Keycloak admin).

Pair the password manager with a hardware key (YubiKey,
Solokey, Titan). These are 1-time setup, $25-50 each, and
make the credentials unstealable in practice -- without the
hardware key, an attacker with your master password still
cannot unlock the vault.

If you lose all of the above, every operator-impossible-to-
replace credential can be re-minted at the upstream provider
(re-mint the CF token at Cloudflare; re-mint the SMTP password
at Brevo; etc.). The only IRRECOVERABLE loss is the restic
password -- which you do not hold and cannot mint, but which
encrypts your backups. Coordinate with your operator if you
suspect that has been compromised.
